Hi all,

dealing now with real observational data I found out that I have to implement a truncation. To make it easier I will focus in the following on a single log-normal and ignore the two component mixture (truncating the mixture model will be hopefully similar).
Therefore, my model should describe log-normally distributed data, which contain a normal measurement uncertainty. Additionally, at very low values or very high values the measurement of the data gets not recorded (the telescope points towards the dens clouds and dilute gas emits only faint light such that the sensitivity is not high enough), such that I have to implement my distributions with truncations. As I want to make a model comparison with different models (mixture model with single log-normal) I have to define the log-likelihood on the data level (as I want to use WAIC - see :
http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/research/unpublished/waic_stan.pdf  ).
With your help my model looks now like:

data {
  int<lower=0>    NumData;
  vector [NumData] Measured_Data;
}

parameters {
   real  <lower=      48, upper=       50 >   LN_mu;
   real  <lower=     0.4, upper=      0.8 >   LN_sigma;
   real  <lower=   +8e19, upper=    +2e20 >   Scatter;
   vector<lower=0> [NumData] Latent_Data;
}

transformed parameters {

  vector<upper=0>[NumData] log_lik;

  for (n in 1:NumData)
  {

      log_lik[n] <-                   lognormal_log (Latent_Data[n]  , LN_mu, LN_sigma);
      log_lik[n] <- log_lik[n] +     normal_log (Measured_Data[n], Latent_Data[n], Scatter);
  }
}

model {

  LN_mu        ~ uniform   (    48,    50);
  LN_sigma   ~ uniform (   0.4,   0.8);
  Scatter        ~ uniform     ( +8e19, +2e20);

  increment_log_prob (sum (log_lik));
}

Which works out. Now I try to implement a truncation, as I have no measurement below and above a certain values. The model now looks like :

data {
  int<lower=0>    NumData;
  real            lower_cut;
  real            upper_cut;
  vector<lower=lower_cut, upper= upper_cut> [NumData] Measured_Data;
}

parameters {
   real  <lower=      48, upper=       50 >   LN_mu;
   real  <lower=     0.4, upper=      0.8 >   LN_sigma;
   real  <lower=   +8e19, upper=    +2e20 >   Scatter;
   vector<lower=  lower_cut, upper= upper_cut > [NumData] Latent_Data;
}

transformed parameters {

  vector<upper=0>[NumData] log_lik;
  real corr_ln;
  real corr_n;

  for (n in 1:NumData)
  {

      log_lik[n] <-                  lognormal_log (Latent_Data[n]  , LN_mu, LN_sigma);
      log_lik[n] <- log_lik[n] +     normal_log (Measured_Data[n], Latent_Data[n], Scatter);

      corr_ln  <- -log_diff_exp(   lognormal_cdf_log((upper_cut), LN_mu, LN_sigma),    lognormal_cdf_log((lower_cut), LN_mu, LN_sigma));
      corr_n   <- -log_diff_exp(   normal_cdf_log(upper_cut, Latent_Data[n], Scatter),    normal_cdf_log(lower_cut, Latent_Data[n], Scatter));

      print("corr_n = ",corr_n, "     corr_ln = ", corr_ln);

      log_lik[n] <- log_lik[n] + corr_ln;
      log_lik[n] <- log_lik[n] + corr_n;
  }
}

model {

  LN_mu      ~ uniform (    48,    50);
  LN_sigma   ~ uniform (   0.4,   0.8);
  Scatter    ~ uniform ( +8e19, +2e20);

  increment_log_prob (sum (log_lik));
}

I truncate both the Measured_Data as well as the  Latent_Data, as I found out that just truncating the Measured_Data results in a distribution with pronounced wings, as every datum of Latent_data outside the measurement range has to result in a measurement inside the range such that the wings of the scatter get overweighted (ie. the area with which I normalise the probability gets tiny.). Truncating both is the best idea  I have to get around this issue. Is there a smarter way?

Beside this, the above stated model results now in the following error independent of if I do define the initial values or not:

Error : Rejecting initial value:
  Gradient evaluated at the initial value is not finite.
  Stan can't start sampling from this initial value.

Do you have an idea how I could go on? Do I have to implement the convolution/numerical integration of the log-normal with the normal and truncate the resulting distribution? Does anyone has experience with this? Is this possible? As I have to calc the convolution for every datapoint this takes forever, does it? Is it possible to parallelise the calculation of the log likelihood. Is there a way of working with OpenMP such that one can use " #pragma omp parallel for" in stan. That would be awesome.

Thanks for your help.
Cheers,
Lukas

