I'm not really following all the physics (for instance why
you have normal error (additive) in a lognormal model (multiplicative).

This:

   real  <lower=   +8e19, upper=    +2e20 >   Scatter;

is going to give you a headache in Stan.  It'll initialze
around 1e20, but the step size is going to have to be adapted
considerably before it'll move, and it'll be huge relative to
the other components.  If there's a way to scale this data back
down to something more unit sized, it'll be much more efficient
to run in Stan.

No, there's no way as of yet to parallelize likelihood.  It's
tricky with autodiff.

For the rejection, you have to make sure that any legal parameter
value according to the constraints has support (finite log density)
in the model.  You can add print() statements to track how far you
get and where things go wrong.

- Bob

